it are used to seeing  food prices two or three times higher  than the national average canada's  northernmost city is inaccessible by  road from the south and its short  growing season means food and supplies  often have to be flown in well now in  response to the inflationary crisis  inuit hunters are stepping up to provide  more food and they've just found a new  lifeline jeff semple reports from  iqaluit  the name acalaweed in inuktitut means  place of many fish and it's easy to see  why  grew up on these waters fishing for  arctic char as well as clams beluga  whales and seals and whatever they can't  eat themselves they share with friends  some of them don't have any hunters in  their family  some of them are elders we care a lot  i'm not the only one the other countries  do that but that long-standing inuit  tradition of sharing the catch has fast  become a lifeline for this remote  northern city  record high inflation has sent food  prices soaring across the country and  nowhere more so than canada's  northernmost communities ocala was  already home to some of the most  expensive food in the country and those  prices are climbing even higher this bag  of potatoes costs about 15  it's almost 22 dollars for a kilogram of  ground beef and a four liter jug of milk  will cost you more than eight dollars  that's two dollars more than the  national average  when it was a kid 10 cents for a pop  today you could  five dollars 25 cents for a problem  hunters like emilio alnaco and his  curious son jonah are now donating their  excess meat to this local food center  distributed daily to those in need  compared to last year demand for food  donations has doubled most of the food  we get is fluent in from south because  we don't have aquaculture it's too cold  up here in the north so we have to look  for other sources to that end this  inuit-owned development company recently  decided to look a little deeper  combining traditional knowledge with  cutting-edge technology 