y the fairness  that he brought to to broadcast scully  was born in the bronx the son of irish  immigrants his ambitions as a baseball  player were never realized he began  broadcasting in 1950 developing a style  that was measured and never rushed  much like the game itself  unlike many colleagues he resisted being  a partisan cheerleader not even for the  los angeles dodgers a team he called  thousands of games for over almost seven  decades of broadcasting if it was the  red sox yankees he didn't focus on the  yankees you know he gave both teams  involved an equal amount of uh of time  and i appreciated that because i  expected it was going to be the other  way around one ball  scully also had a keen sense of  baseball's place in society like in 1974  when henry aaron broke babe ruth's  all-time home run record what a  marvelous moment for the country in the  world  a black man is getting a standing  ovation in the deep south for breaking a  record of an all-time baseball idol  every broadcaster i've ever asked who's  the greatest baseball broadcaster of all  time they've said scully without  exception it was a big moment he'd let  the crowd reaction take over for his  voice  [Applause]  along with the presidential medal of  freedom scully was honored with an emmy  award and of course induction to the  baseball hall of fame scully's wife  sandra died last year he was a father of  five children and dozens of  grandchildren and great-grandchildren at  age 94 vin scully's elegant excellence  is unmatched ross lord global news  and that's global national for this  wednesday night i'm farah nasser and on  behalf of our whole crew i want to thank  you so much for spending part of your  evening with us tonight's york canada is  the inukshuk overlooking kluwani lake in  yukon  until tomorrow take care of yourselves  and each other good night  [Music]  you
 

